issue.description:
## Task
Add pipeline timing and a 5-iteration rolling average to `ProcessorManager` in `mothership/internal/signal/processor.go`.

## Changes to ProcessorManager struct (lines 221-228)
Add these fields:
```go
type ProcessorManager struct {
    // ... existing fields ...
    iterDurations [5]time.Duration  // ring buffer for last 5 iteration times
    iterIdx       int               // next write index (mod 5)
    iterCount     int               // how many values filled (0-5)
    shedLevel     int               // current load shedding level (0-3)
    steadyCount   int               // consecutive iters below recovery threshold
}
```

## Changes to LinkProcessor.Process() (line 54)
Wrap the entire Process body to time it. At the START of Process():
```go
t0 := time.Now()
```
At the END of Process(), before return, update the manager's ring buffer. BUT — Process is on LinkProcessor, not ProcessorManager. So instead, add timing to ProcessorManager.Process() (line 252):

```go
func (pm *ProcessorManager) Process(linkID string, ...) (*ProcessResult, error) {
    t0 := time.Now()
    // ... existing lock + delegate to processor ...
    result, err := processor.Process(payload, rssiDBm, nSub, recvTime)
    pm.mu.Unlock()  // already have write lock
    elapsed := time.Since(t0)
    pm.updateShedding(elapsed)
    return result, err
}
```

## New method updateShedding(elapsed time.Duration)
```go
func (pm *ProcessorManager) updateShedding(elapsed time.Duration) {
    pm.iterDurations[pm.iterIdx%5] = elapsed
    pm.iterIdx++
    if pm.iterCount < 5 { pm.iterCount++ }

    // compute rolling avg
    var sum time.Duration
    for i := 0; i < pm.iterCount; i++ {
        sum += pm.iterDurations[i]
    }
    avg := sum / time.Duration(pm.iterCount)

    // level up
    if avg >= 95*time.Millisecond && pm.shedLevel < 3 {
        pm.shedLevel = 3; pm.steadyCount = 0
    } else if avg >= 90*time.Millisecond && pm.shedLevel < 2 {
        pm.shedLevel = 2; pm.steadyCount = 0
    } else if avg >= 80*time.Millisecond && pm.shedLevel < 1 {
        pm.shedLevel = 1; pm.steadyCount = 0
    }

    // recovery: step down one level when avg < 60ms for 10 consecutive iters
    if avg < 60*time.Millisecond {
        pm.steadyCount++
        if pm.steadyCount >= 10 && pm.shedLevel > 0 {
            pm.shedLevel--
            pm.steadyCount = 0
        }
    } else {
        pm.steadyCount = 0
    }
}
```

## New getter
```go
func (pm *ProcessorManager) GetShedLevel() int {
    pm.mu.RLock()
    defer pm.mu.RUnlock()
    return pm.shedLevel
}
```

Note: `updateShedding` must NOT hold `pm.mu` because it's called after unlock. The iterDurations ring buffer is only written from `Process` so it is already serialized by the caller's lock sequence. Add a separate `mu` for the shed state or call updateShedding while still holding pm.mu — simplest: call it BEFORE Unlock, while still holding the write lock.

## Verify
```bash
cd /home/coding/spaxel/mothership && PATH=$PATH:/home/coding/go/bin go build ./...
```

mothership/internal/volume/shape.go zoneMatchesVolume() (~line 910-916), used by the predicted_enter path (Component 23 Presence Prediction & Pre-emptive Automation, docs/plan/plan.md ~line 1807-1858, and Component 18 Spatial Automation Builder) to decide whether a predicted next-zone matches a configured trigger volume, has this TODO and implementation:

    // TODO: Add zone-to-volume mapping; for now, we trigger if the predicted zone name
    // is in the trigger name or if the trigger name contains the predicted zone name
    if !s.zoneMatchesVolume(pred.PredictedNextZoneName, t) { continue }
    ...
    // zoneMatchesVolume checks if a zone name matches a trigger volume.
    // TODO: Implement proper zone-to-volume geometry mapping.
    func (s *Store) zoneMatchesVolume(zoneName string, t *Trigger) bool {
        // For now, use simple string matching
        ...

This means whether a pre-emptive automation (e.g. "turn on kitchen lights before Alice arrives") actually fires is determined by whether the predicted zone NAME happens to be a substring of the trigger volume NAME (or vice versa) -- not by whether the predicted zone geometrically overlaps the trigger volume. Renaming a zone or trigger (e.g. "Kitchen" trigger vs a predicted zone literally named "Kitchen Counter") changes behavior in ways that have nothing to do with actual physical space, and a trigger volume that does not share zone-naming conventions with the prediction system will never fire via this path regardless of correct geometric containment.

No open or in-progress bead currently tracks this (confirmed via bf search).

Fix: replace the substring match with a real geometric test -- resolve the predicted zone name to its Zone record (position/bounds, per the zones table used elsewhere in this package/mothership/internal/api/zones.go) and check actual overlap/containment against t.Shape (the same IsInside/geometry already used by evaluateEnter for live blobs), rather than string comparison. Add a unit test in mothership/internal/volume covering: a trigger volume that geometrically contains a zone fires regardless of naming mismatch; a trigger volume with a matching name but no geometric overlap does NOT fire. Verify with: cd mothership && go test ./internal/volume/... -run ZoneMatchesVolume -v (or the closest existing test name for this function).

